  "llm_reasoning_text": "\n## Index Tuning Analysis \u2014 TPC-C Workload\n\n### Queries Already Served by Existing PKs (no new index needed)\n- **Q1** \u2192 `warehouse_pkey (w_id)` \u2014 point lookup, trivial.\n- **Q2** \u2192 `district_pkey (d_w_id, d_id)` \u2014 point lookup, trivial.\n- **Q3** \u2192 `customer_pkey (c_w_id, c_d_id, c_id)` \u2014 point lookup, trivial.\n- **Q4** \u2192 `item_pkey (i_id)` \u2014 point lookup, trivial.\n- **Q5** \u2192 `stock_pkey (s_w_id, s_i_id)` \u2014 point lookup, trivial.\n- **Q8** \u2192 `order_line_pkey (ol_w_id, ol_d_id, ol_o_id, ol_number)` \u2014 prefix scan, trivial.\n- **Q9** \u2192 `new_order_pkey (no_w_id, no_d_id, no_o_id)` \u2014 index-only scan with LIMIT 1; cost = **0.32**, already optimal.\n- **Q10** \u2192 `order_line_pkey` \u2014 prefix scan on `(ol_w_id, ol_d_id, ol_o_id)`, trivial.\n\n### New Index 1: `customer(c_w_id, c_d_id, c_last, c_first)`\n- **Targets**: Q6 (and also benefits Q12's nested-loop inner side as a covering index).\n- **Q6** filters on `(c_w_id=1, c_d_id=3, c_last='BARBARANTI')` and orders by `c_first`. Without this index the planner falls back to a bitmap heap scan + sort (~372). With this index the planner uses a forward **Index Scan** on the three equality columns (`c_w_id`, `c_d_id`, `c_last`), with `c_first` already in the index for ordering, collapsing cost to **8.43**.\n- Column order rationale: `(c_w_id, c_d_id)` are the high-selectivity warehouse/district prefix; `c_last` is the equality filter; `c_first` is the trailing sort key, enabling the index to deliver rows in the right order without a separate sort step.\n\n### New Index 2: `orders(o_w_id, o_d_id, o_c_id, o_id)`\n- **Targets**: Q7 (and supports Q12 join lookups).\n- **Q7** needs `(o_w_id=1, o_d_id=3, o_c_id=42)` with `ORDER BY o_id DESC LIMIT 1`. Without this index, the planner uses a bitmap heap scan on the PK prefix `(o_w_id, o_d_id)` and then filters + sorts, costing ~372. With this index the planner does a single backward **Index Scan** on all three equality columns and immediately returns the first (maximum) `o_id`, costing **8.31**.\n- Column order rationale: `(o_w_id, o_d_id, o_c_id)` are equality predicates; `o_id` as the trailing column allows the index to serve the `ORDER BY o_id DESC LIMIT 1` directly via a backward scan, eliminating any sort.\n\n### New Index 3: `stock(s_w_id, s_quantity, s_i_id)`\n- **Targets**: Q11.\n- **Q11** joins `order_line` (range scan on `ol_o_id` 980\u2013999) with `stock` where `s_w_id=1` and `s_quantity < 15`, then counts distinct `s_i_id`. Without any special stock index the planner does a nested-loop with `stock_pkey` and applies `s_quantity < 15` as a filter, costing ~1843. With `(s_w_id, s_quantity, s_i_id)` the planner switches to a **Hash Join** strategy: it does an **Index Only Scan** on stock using `(s_w_id=1, s_quantity < 15)` as the index condition (reading only `s_i_id` from the index, no heap access), then hash-joins against the order_line range scan, reducing cost to **~650\u2013678** \u2014 a ~63% reduction.\n- Column order rationale: `s_w_id` is the equality prefix; `s_quantity` enables the range filter `< 15` to be pushed into the index condition; `s_i_id` is the payload needed by the query, making this a covering (index-only) scan for stock.\n\n### Q12 \u2014 No new index recommended\nQ12 aggregates `SUM(ol_amount)` across all orders for all customers at `c_w_id=1`, joining all three large tables. The planner consistently prefers seq scans on `orders` (30 000 rows) and `customer` (30 000 rows) for the inner hash build, regardless of indexes tested. This is correct behavior: when nearly all rows of a warehouse are fetched, an index would cost more than a seq scan. The existing `order_line_pkey` already efficiently serves the per-order line lookups inside that query. No additional index materially reduces Q12's cost.\n",
